
5d-Block Quadruple-Zeta Basis Set Archive
=========================================


Date: 13 Nov 2009


When publishing work that uses these basis sets, please use the following citation:

K.G. Dyall, Theor. Chem. Acc. (2007) 117:483.
Available from the Dirac web site, http://dirac.chem.sdu.dk.



This archive contains the exponents and the SCF spinor or orbital coefficients,
given with respect to normalized (spherical or 2-spinor) Gaussian functions, for
the quadruple-zeta basis sets described in the above article. The primitive
basis set is a 34s 30p 19d 12f set, including functions for the 6p.

NOTE: Functions are counted from the function with the smallest exponent to the largest. 

The SCF exponent sets were generated as follows:

* Optimization of the s, p, d, and f sets separately, on the total energy of the
  configuration average of the d^n s^2 and d^n+1 s^1 configurations
* Addition of 4 exponents for the 6p, optimized with reoptimization of the
  exponents for the 3p-5p outer antinodes on the total energy of the d^n s^2
  configuration
* Replacement of the outer three SCF f exponent with the three 5d/6s correlating f
  exponents and reoptimization of the other 9 f exponents.

In all the following descriptions, this is the set referred to as the SCF 
exponent set. 

The correlating exponents were generated as follows

* A 3f2g1h set for the 5d and 6s was optimized in MR-SDCI calculations on the
  ground configuration.
* A 3g2h1i set for the 4f and was optimized in MR-DCI calculations on the
  ground configuration, with double excitations from the 4f shell into the 
  correlating orbitals coupled to J=0.

The exponents for 5d dipole polarization were generated as follows:

* The f set was extended in an even-tempered fashion to produce an f exponent
  for dipole polarization. The ratio used to obtain this f exponent was applied
  to the g correlating exponent to obtain a g exponent for dipole polarization.

* The f and g sets were extended in an even-tempered fashion to produce f and g
  exponents for dipole polarization. The ratio used to obtain the g exponent was
  applied to the h correlating exponent to obtain an h exponent for dipole
  polarization.

The contractions are taken from SCF calculations on the weighted average of
the d^n s^2, d^n+1 s^1, and d^n p^2 configurations with weight ratio
45%:45%:10%, except for Hg, where the d^n s^2 and d^n p^2 configurations are
used with weight ratio 90%:10%.


The archive is set out as follows.

1. Exponents and SCF coefficients for Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave functions, in
   order of Z. The large component functions are given first, followed by the
   small component functions. Both components are labelled with the large
   component symmetry type, but the primitive functions for the small component
   are those obtained by restricted kinetic balance from the large component.
   The spin-orbit components have a "-" label for j=l-1/2 and a "+" label for
   j=l+1/2.

2. Exponents and SCF coefficients for spin-free modified Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave
   functions, in order of Z. The large component functions are given first, 
   followed by the pseudo-large component functions, then the Foldy-Wouthuysen
   transformed large component functions. Note that all components have the same
   symmetry type. 

3. Exponents of valence correlating functions. The f functions are included in
   the SCF set. 

4. Exponents of valence dipole polarization functions.

5. Exponents of 5s5p inner valence correlating g functions.

6. Exponents of 4f outer core correlating functions.



Basis set construction.  
----------------------

Contracted basis sets can be constructed by adding primitive functions to the
SCF set. These basis sets are described below. Note that the term "quadruple
zeta" applies to the s and p set, and the d set is quintuple zeta. This is a
result of keeping a reasonable energy balance for the basis sets. Functions are
counted in increasing exponent size, starting from the smallest.

The valence space is defined here as the 5d, 6s, and 6p orbitals, the inner
valence space is defined as the 5s and 5p orbitals, and the outer core space as
the 4f orbital.

1. Valence flexibility. To the SCF functions, add the following:

   1st, 2nd, and 4th s primitives from the SCF set 
   1st, 2nd, and 3rd p primitives from the SCF set
   1st through 4th d primitives from the SCF set
   1st through 3rd f primitives from the SCF set (valence correlating functions)
   Valence correlating 2g1h functions

2. Valence + inner valence. This set adds the inner valence correlation from the
   5s and 5p shells. To set 1, add the following functions:

   5th, 6th, and 8th s primitives from the SCF set 
   5th, 6th, and 8th p primitives from the SCF set
   5th and 6th d primitives from the SCF set
   4th f primitive from the SCF set
   5s5p correlating g function

   If linear dependence problems are encountered in the d space, use one of the
   following solutions:
   * Do not add the 3rd d primitive (loss of up to 0.8 mEh in the valence correlation
     energy).
   * Add the 5th and 7th d primitives instead of the 5th and 6th (loss of about 1 mEh 
     in the 5s5p correlation energy).

3. Valence + inner valence + outer core. This set adds functions for the correlation
   of the 4f orbital. To set 2, add the following functions:

   9th, 10th, and 11th s primitives from the SCF set 
   10th, 11th, and 12th p primitives from the SCF set
   7th, 8th, and 9th d primitives from the SCF set
   5th and 7th f primitives from the SCF set

   This set should also be suitable for correlation of the 4d, 4p and 4s.

4. Dipole polarization of the 5d. To any of the above sets, add the dipole
   polarization 1f1g1h set.





